Troubleshooting Containers
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Diagnose container failures using logs and inspect commands
View container logs with filters
Inspect container state
Use exec to debug inside a running container
Prerequisites
Basic understanding of command-line interface (CLI)
Podman or Docker installed (Podman recommended for OpenShift compatibility)
A running container (we'll create one for the lab)
Lab Setup
Install Podman (if not already installed):

sudo dnf install -y podman  # For RHEL/CentOS/Fedora
sudo apt-get install -y podman  # For Debian/Ubuntu
Pull a sample container image:

podman pull docker.io/library/nginx:alpine
Run a test container:

podman run -d --name nginx-test -p 8080:80 nginx:alpine
Task 1: View Container Logs with Filters
Subtask 1.1: View Basic Logs
Check container logs:

podman logs nginx-test
Expected Output: Displays the Nginx access/error logs.
View logs in real-time:

podman logs -f nginx-test
Expected Outcome: Logs stream continuously until you press Ctrl+C.
Subtask 1.2: Filter Logs by Time
View logs since a specific time:

podman logs --since 5m nginx-test
Explanation: Shows logs from the last 5 minutes.
View last N lines:

podman logs --tail 10 nginx-test
Expected Outcome: Displays the last 10 log entries.
Troubleshooting Tip
If logs are empty, ensure the container is running (podman ps).
Task 2: Inspect Container State
Subtask 2.1: Check Container Status
List running containers:

podman ps
Expected Output: Shows nginx-test in the list.
Inspect detailed container state:

podman inspect nginx-test
Key Fields to Observe:
State": Running, ExitCode, Error
Config: Environment variables, entrypoint
NetworkSettings: IP address, ports
Subtask 2.2: Check Resource Usage
View container stats:
podman stats nginx-test
Expected Outcome: Live CPU, memory, and network usage.
Troubleshooting Tip
If a container is stuck, use podman stop and restart it.
Task 3: Use exec to Debug Inside Container
Subtask 3.1: Enter Container Shell
Open an interactive shell:

podman exec -it nginx-test /bin/sh
Explanation: This drops you into the container's shell.
Check running processes:

ps aux
Expected Output: Shows Nginx and other processes.
Subtask 3.2: Debug Service Issues
Check Nginx config:

cat /etc/nginx/nginx.conf
Expected Outcome: Displays the Nginx configuration.
Test Nginx connectivity:

curl localhost
Expected Output: Returns the default Nginx welcome page.
Troubleshooting Tip
If /bin/sh fails, try /bin/bash (if available in the container).
Conclusion
In this lab, you learned how to:

View and filter container logs for debugging.
Inspect container state and resource usage.
Use exec to troubleshoot inside a running environment.
Next Steps
Practice debugging a misconfigured container (e.g., one that crashes on startup).
Explore podman system df to check disk usage by containers.
Cleanup
podman stop nginx-test
podman rm nginx-test
